. Co-activation probe: On monolingual target-language QA/generation, at each layer apply logit-lens and trained linear probes to detect the dominant-language (English) answer token. Metric: layer-wise decodability (probe accuracy / logit-lens rank of English answer) while output is verified to be in the target language. Compare across 20+ languages spanning Latin/Cyrillic/Arabic/CJK/Devanagari and 4+ families. Baseline: random/unrelated-language answer decodability. 2. Suppression trajectory & Suppression Cost: track the English competitor token's logit across late layers; define Suppression Cost = drop in competitor logit rank from peak-activation layer to output. Test whether the competitor is actively pushed down (suppression) vs. simply never boosted. 3. Localize suppression mechanism: use attention-head attribution and activation patching to find heads/MLP directions responsible for damping the competitor; ablate them and measure (a) rise in competitor logit, (b) Leakage Rate = fraction of outputs that switch to the dominant language or code-switch. 4. Distance dose-response: correlate co-activation strength and Suppression Cost with lang2vec typological distance and a same-script binary indicator; test monotonic scaling (Spearman). Prediction: distant/different-script languages show higher co-activation and higher suppression cost/leakage. 5. Recover a 'suppression direction' (mean activation difference between clean monolingual and leakage-prone runs); at inference, steer along it on low-resource languages. Metrics: Leakage Rate reduction, output-language ID accuracy, fluency (perplexity), and task accuracy — verifying we reduce leakage without harming correctness. 6. Ablations: model scale, dominant-language-forced vs in-language prompting, and whether the competitor is always English or the highest-resource typologically-nearest language (tests whether suppression targets the *dominant* vs *nearest* language). Models: Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, Qwen2-7B.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1. The 'competitor' might be decodable simply because the concept space is shared, not because of genuine co-activation of a specific language's token; we control by comparing dominant-language decodability against unrelated languages and by testing whether it is actively suppressed (dynamic drop) rather than merely present. 2. Logit-lens/probe decodability can reflect the readout basis rather than a used representation; we require the causal ablation (leakage induction) to confirm functional relevance. 3. Suppression may be diffuse rather than localized to identifiable heads, weakening the circuit story; we report degree of localization honestly and fall back on a direction-based (not head-based) account. 4. Leakage may be rare in strong models, limiting statistical power; we include low-resource languages and adversarial prompts to elicit it. 5. Distinguishing 'dominant language' from 'typologically nearest high-resource language' as the suppression target requires careful language selection; experiment 6 is designed for this. 6. Findings may vary across model families; three families address but do not exhaust generality."}

