Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "cultural_frame_register",
  "Title": "Which Answer Is True Here? A Mechanistic Study of How Multilingual LLMs Select Among Culturally-Relative Truths",
  "Short Hypothesis": "For claims whose truth value is genuinely relative to a cultural/geographic frame (e.g., 'people drive on the left', 'the head of state is a monarch', 'the school year starts in spring'), multilingual LLMs store multiple mutually-contradictory candidate answers simultaneously and select among them via an internal 'frame register' set in mid layers. We hypothesize that (a) the query language acts as an implicit, weak frame selector that competes with explicit textual frame cues, (b) this selection is mediated by a small, causally-identifiable set of directions/components separable from the answer-content computation, and (c) selection failures (giving the answer correct for the wrong frame) scale with typological/script distance between the query language and the language canonically associated with the intended frame.", "Related Work": "Recent interpretability work localizes 'culture neurons' separable from language neurons (Isolating Culture Neurons 2025), finds cultural localization in middle layers steerable by cultural vectors (Localized Cultural Knowledge is Conserved 2025), documents a Cultural-Linguistic Synergy where in-language cultural queries activate more specialized neurons (Disentangling Language and Culture 2025), shows cross-lingual alignment causes cultural erasure fixable by layer-specific Surgical Steering (Rethinking Cross-lingual Alignment 2025), and finds internal paths are more language- than content-driven (Language over Content 2025). Factual-storage work (Tracing the Roots of Facts 2024; Lost in Multilinguality 2025) categorizes facts as shared/independent and finds a language-agnostic concept space with late-layer decoding. All of this treats cultural knowledge as static localized content or measures bias toward one canonical (often Western) answer. None studies claims with NO single correct answer\u2014where multiple contradictory answers are simultaneously true under different frames\u2014nor characterizes the mechanism that *selects* among stored competing answers as a function of an explicit-vs-implicit (language) frame cue. We reframe cultural knowledge as a frame-conditioned selection problem and provide the first causal account of a 'frame register' plus a diagnostic of when language overrides explicit context.", "Abstract": "Many claims about the world have no universal truth value: whether people drive on the left, whether the head of state is a monarch, or when the school year begins depends on the cultural or geographic frame in which the question is asked. Multilingual LLMs must therefore not merely recall facts but *select* which stored, frame-dependent answer applies. We investigate this selection mechanism. We construct FRAME-REL, a controlled dataset of culturally-relative claims for which several countries have known, mutually-contradictory ground-truth answers, and probe models under two orthogonal frame cues: an explicit textual cue (naming the country) and an implicit cue (the query language). We first show that multiple contradictory candidate answers are simultaneously linearly decodable from mid-layer hidden states before selection collapses to one output, evidencing parallel storage rather than a single canonical answer. Using activation patching, we localize a compact 'frame register'\u2014mid-layer components that, when swapped between a same-question run under frame A and under frame B, flip the selected answer without altering answer content. We then quantify a competition between explicit and implicit (language) cues: how often does asking in language L override an explicit country cue, and does this override scale with typological/script distance between L and the frame's canonical language? Experiments span 15+ languages and Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, and Qwen2-7B. We further test a training-free intervention that steers the frame register to fix mismatched selections without harming fluency, and compare against explicit-prompting baselines. The work recasts multilingual cultural knowledge as frame-conditioned selection and delivers a causal, controllable account.", "Experiments": "1. Dataset (FRAME-REL): assemble culturally-relative claim templates with verifiable per-country ground truth that differs across countries (driving side, monarchy vs republic, currency, primary school start season, calendar/new-year timing, measurement units, dominant religion, plug voltage, tipping norms). For each claim, collect >=6 countries with contradictory answers and their canonical languages. Render each claim in 15+ query languages spanning Latin/Cyrillic/Arabic/CJK/Devanagari and 4+ families, crossed with explicit country cues (present/absent). Models: Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, Qwen2-7B. 2. Parallel-storage probe: for each claim, apply logit-lens and trained linear probes at each layer to test whether multiple countries' answers are simultaneously decodable in mid layers before the output answer emerges. Metric: layer-wise decodability rank of each candidate answer; report co-decodability of contradictory answers vs. a single-answer factual baseline. 3. Locate the frame register: run the identical question under explicit frame A (country A) and frame B (country B), producing different answers. Activation-patch mid-layer components from the A-run into the B-run; identify the minimal set whose patching flips the selected answer to A's answer. Metric: flip rate vs. number/location of patched components; verify content (which candidates exist) is preserved while selection changes. 4. Explicit-vs-implicit (language) competition: with the explicit country cue set to country C but the query posed in language L (canonical language of a different country D), measure how often the output matches C (explicit wins), D (language wins), or neither. Metric: language-override rate. 5. Distance dose-response: correlate language-override rate and frame-selection error with lang2vec typological distance and same-script indicator between L and the intended frame's canonical language (Spearman). Prediction: override/error rises with distance. 6. Causal steering intervention: extract a 'frame direction' per country from mean activation differences at the frame register; at inference, steer the register to the intended frame on mismatched items. Metrics: selection-correction rate, output fluency (perplexity), answer-content preservation, and comparison against explicit-country-prompt and translate-to-English baselines. 7. Separability check: test whether the frame register overlaps with previously reported language-specific and culture-specific neurons (compute overlap) to establish it as a distinct selection (not storage) mechanism. 8. Ablations: model scale; register layer sweep; single-answer factual controls (should show no parallel storage / no flippable register); robustness of probes across models (train on one, test on another). Metrics throughout: candidate decodability rank, flip rate, language-override rate, Spearman with distance, steering-correction rate, fluency, neuron-overlap.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1. Ground truth for culturally-relative claims can be ambiguous or change over time; we restrict to unambiguous, stable, verifiable claims (driving side, monarchy status, plug voltage) and have annotators validate per-country answers. 2. Logit-lens/probe decodability of multiple candidates may reflect the readout basis rather than functional parallel storage; we require the causal patching flip (Exp. 3) to confirm functional relevance. 3. The frame register may be diffuse rather than compact, weakening the localization story; we report degree of localization honestly and fall back to a direction-based account. 4. Some models may simply always default to the canonical/Western answer regardless of frame, collapsing selection; even so, quantifying the near-absence of frame sensitivity and its language dependence is a contribution, and multiple models mitigate this. 5. Tokenization differences across scripts complicate cross-language probing and answer matching; we restrict to claims with clean, short answer tokens and use last-token-of-answer representations. 6. Steering may trade content preservation for correct selection; we report both. 7. Findings may be model-specific; three families address but do not exhaust generality."
}}
