Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "tense_grammaticalization_gap", "Title": "When the Grammar Is Silent: How Multilingual LLMs Represent Time in Tenseless vs. Tensed Languages", "Short Hypothesis": "Multilingual LLMs build a shared, language-agnostic 'reference-time' representation (the abstract past/present/future ordering a sentence describes) that is distinct from the surface tense-morphology representation. We hypothesize that (a) for morphologically tensed languages the model can lazily read reference time off surface tense features, while for tenseless/aspect-only languages (Chinese, Indonesian, Yoruba) it must infer reference time from lexical/pragmatic context using a slower, later-layer, more error-prone circuit; (b) this creates a measurable 'grammaticalization gap' in the robustness and localization of the abstract time representation that tracks whether a language grammatically marks tense, NOT its resource level; and (c) the abstract reference-time direction transfers across languages, so it can be causally read from a tensed language and injected to repair temporal inference in a tenseless one.", "Related Work": "Prior probing work shows tense is easy to decode in morphologically-marked languages (French) but hard in Chinese, attributing the drop to memorization vs. abstraction (Multilingual Probe for Tense 2021), and that transformers encode aspect features like telicity/duration (About Time 2022; Verbal Aspect 2024). Recent interpretability shows grammatical concepts (number, gender, tense) share cross-lingual feature directions (Latent Grammatical Concepts 2025) and that tense/aspect are steerable orthogonal directions (Steering Tense and Aspect 2025). LLMs also encode linear time and 'temporal heads' for factual dates (Language Models Represent Space and Time 2023; Temporal Heads 2025). Cross-linguistic NLI work shows LLMs struggle with perfect-aspect temporal inference in Chinese/Japanese (2025). However, ALL prior work either (i) probes surface tense morphology, or (ii) studies factual event dates, and treats the tenseless-language decoding drop as a probing artifact. None distinguishes the abstract SENTENCE-INTERNAL reference-time representation (the situation's temporal location, present regardless of whether grammar marks it) from surface tense morphology, nor tests whether tenseless languages recruit a distinct, later, error-prone inference circuit, nor uses a tensed language as a causal donor to repair temporal inference in a tenseless one. We reframe the classic French-vs-Chinese gap as a mechanistic grammaticalization-gap phenomenon with a causal cross-lingual fix.", "Abstract": "Some languages grammatically mark when an event happened (English 'walked' vs 'walks'); others, like Chinese, Indonesian, and Yoruba, are largely tenseless and leave temporal location to be inferred from adverbs, aspect markers, and pragmatics. Yet any comprehending model must recover the same abstract reference time. We ask how multilingual LLMs represent this abstract sentence-internal time, and whether the mechanism differs when grammar is silent. We distinguish two representations: surface tense morphology (which verb form appears) and abstract reference time (past/present/future location of the described situation). Using parallel sentences whose reference time is fixed by context across tensed and tenseless languages, we train linear probes and locate the layers and components that encode reference time in each. We predict a 'grammaticalization gap': in tensed languages reference time is decodable early and localizes with surface-tense features, whereas in tenseless languages it emerges only in later layers via a distinct, more error-prone circuit, and this gap tracks whether a language marks tense rather than its resource level (e.g., low-resource tensed Swahili patterns with English, high-resource tenseless Chinese with Indonesian). We causally test whether the abstract reference-time direction is shared: extracting it from a tensed language and steering it into a tenseless-language forward pass should repair temporal-inference errors without harming fluency. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, and Qwen2-7B and 12+ languages of both types. This recasts the classic tense-probing gap as a controllable, cross-lingually transferable mechanistic phenomenon.", "Experiments": "1. Stimuli: build parallel minimal-pair sentences where the abstract reference time (past/present/future) is unambiguously fixed by an explicit temporal adverbial ('yesterday'/'now'/'tomorrow') and by a bare-context version (no adverbial, inferable only from lexical/pragmatic cues). Render in 12+ languages split into TENSED (English, Spanish, Russian, Swahili [low-resource but tensed], Hindi) and TENSELESS/aspect-only (Chinese, Indonesian, Yoruba, Thai, Vietnamese). Annotate surface tense-morphology label and abstract reference-time label separately. Models: Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, Qwen2-7B. 2. Two-target probing: train separate linear probes for (a) surface tense morphology and (b) abstract reference time, per layer, per language. Metric: probe accuracy vs layer; identify emergence layer (first layer >90% or peak) for reference time in each language. Prediction: tensed languages peak early and reference-time probe correlates with surface-tense probe; tenseless languages peak later and dissociate. 3. Grammaticalization-gap quantification: define gap = (reference-time emergence layer / robustness in bare-context condition) difference between tenseless and tensed languages. Correlate gap with a binary 'grammatically marks tense' typology indicator vs a resource-level proxy (pretraining share / mLAMA accuracy). Spearman with CIs; key contrast: low-resource tensed (Swahili) vs high-resource tenseless (Chinese) to dissociate typology from resource. 4. Circuit localization: use activation patching / attention-head attribution to find components that carry reference time in tensed vs tenseless languages; measure overlap (Jaccard) between the two circuits and whether tenseless languages recruit additional later components tied to adverbial/context tokens. 5. Shared-direction test & causal transfer: extract abstract reference-time direction (mean activation difference past vs future) from a TENSED donor language; test cross-lingual decodability (donor probe applied to tenseless language) and, crucially, steer the donor-derived direction into a tenseless-language forward pass on bare-context temporal-inference items where the model errs. Metrics: temporal-NLI accuracy gain (using Chinese/Japanese perfect-aspect NLI style items and our bare-context set), output fluency (perplexity), and specificity (no change to unrelated features). Compare to a same-language steering baseline and a translate-to-English baseline. 6. Behavioral link: on a temporal-inference task (order two events / answer when-question), test that low reference-time probe confidence in tenseless bare-context items predicts higher error, beyond the true label. 7. Ablations: adverbial-present vs bare-context; layer sweep; model scale; aspect-marker presence (does an aspect marker in Chinese restore early decodability?); probe-transfer robustness across models. Metrics throughout: probe accuracy, emergence layer, Jaccard circuit overlap, Spearman with typology vs resource, cross-lingual probe transfer accuracy, steering-repair rate, fluency, specificity.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1. Probe decodability may reflect the readout basis rather than a used representation; we require the causal steering-repair (Exp. 5) and the behavioral link (Exp. 6) to establish functional relevance. 2. Separating 'abstract reference time' from 'surface tense' is hard in tensed languages where they nearly coincide; the tenseless languages and the bare-context condition are the crucial dissociating cases and are designed for exactly this. 3. Ground-truth reference time in bare-context tenseless sentences can be genuinely ambiguous; we restrict to items validated by native-speaker annotators with high agreement. 4. Tokenization/script differences complicate cross-language probing; we use clean sentence-final or verb-token representations and report tokenization statistics. 5. Some models may simply route everything through English tense, collapsing the tenseless circuit into a translation step; even so, showing this (via the shared-direction transfer) is a positive, informative result. 6. The typology-vs-resource dissociation hinges on carefully chosen languages (Swahili tensed low-resource, Chinese tenseless high-resource); we report robustness to alternative language choices. 7. Findings may be model-specific; three families address but do not exhaust generality."}}
