er (Slavic/Germanic/Romance vs. English/Chinese controls) and not with resource level. Finally, we recover a shared concept-gender direction and apply a training-free projection that removes leakage, measuring restored cross-lingual consistency without harming fluency. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, and Qwen2-7B across 8+ languages. The work reveals a subtle language-specific contaminant of the supposedly shared concept space and provides a mechanistic, controllable account.", 'Experiments': "1. Stimuli: assemble ~300 inanimate concrete nouns with attested grammatical gender across 6 gendered languages spanning the strength of gender marking (Romance: Spanish, French, Italian; Germanic: German [3-gender]; Slavic: Russian, Polish) plus 2 gender-neutral controls (English, Chinese). Crucially select the subset of concepts whose grammatical gender is OPPOSITE across at least two languages (e.g., key/bridge/sun/moon), giving matched minimal pairs that dissociate grammatical from any semantic gender. Annotate per-language grammatical gender. Models: Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, Qwen2-7B. 2. Behavioral leakage test: pose semantic-gender judgment tasks with the answer forced into a gender-NEUTRAL output language (English/Chinese): 'Would you describe [concept, introduced in language L] as more [masculine-associated adjective] or more [feminine-associated adjective]?', personification pronoun choice ('If this object were a person, would it be he or she?'), and adjective-fit ranking. Metric: rate at which the chosen attribute matches the query language's GRAMMATICAL gender; compute cross-lingual inconsistency = fraction of opposite-gender pairs where the judgment flips with the query language. Baselines: English/Chinese query (no grammatical gender). 3. Concept-gender probe: train linear probes at each layer to decode grammatical gender of the concept from the mid-layer hidden state at the concept-token position (or last token of a neutral-language prompt), using held-out nouns. Show the decoded 'concept-gender' inherits the query language's assignment and is distinct from surface agreement features (control probe on languages with no gender should be near chance). Report layer-wise decodability. 4. Causal patching (core test): for an opposite-gender pair concept, run two forward passes introducing the concept in a masculine-marking language vs a feminine-marking language, both answering in English. Activation-patch the mid-layer concept representation (or the isolated concept-gender direction) from the masculine run into the feminine run; measure whether the English semantic judgment flips to match the donor's grammatical gender while concept identity (topic/answer content) is preserved. Metric: flip rate vs. number/location of patched components; identity-preservation check. 5. Grammaticalization dose-response: correlate per-language leakage (behavioral flip rate + probe decodability) with a gender-marking-strength index (number of genders, agreement pervasiveness) and, as a dissociation, with a resource-level proxy. Prediction: leakage tracks grammaticalization strength, not resource level; English/Chinese controls near zero. Spearman with CIs. 6. Training-free debiasing: extract a shared concept-gender direction (mean activation difference masculine vs feminine, pooled across gendered languages) and project it out of the concept representation at inference. Metrics: reduction in cross-lingual inconsistency, restoration of neutral-baseline judgment distribution, output fluency (perplexity), and specificity (unrelated attributes unchanged). Compare against embedding-debiasing-style baselines and a translate-to-English-first baseline. 7. Ablations: layer sweep for where concept-gender is malleable; single-direction projection vs full-subspace removal; model scale; whether German's neuter provides a third pole; probe transfer across models (train on one, test on another); and a semantically-gendered control set (man/woman/king/queen) that SHOULD retain gender to verify the intervention is selective to grammatical leakage. Metrics throughout: attribute-match rate, cross-lingual flip/inconsistency rate, probe accuracy, causal flip rate, identity preservation, Spearman with grammaticalization vs resource, debiasing consistency gain, fluency, specificity.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1. Probe decodability of concept-gender may reflect the readout basis rather than a functionally used representation; we require the causal patching flip (Exp. 4) and behavioral debiasing (Exp. 6) to establish functional relevance. 2. Some 'semantic gender' judgments may reflect genuine cultural stereotypes rather than grammatical leakage; the opposite-gender minimal pairs and gender-neutral control languages are designed to isolate the grammatical contribution, and we control for stereotype associations of the target concepts. 3. Leakage may be small in strong models that fully abstract away grammar—still an informative positive result (concept space is genuinely clean), and multiple languages/models give statistical power. 4. Tokenization differences across languages/scripts complicate concept-token localization; we use last-token-of-concept or neutral-prompt final-token representations and report tokenization statistics. 5. Debiasing may over-remove and harm legitimately gendered concepts; the semantically-gendered control set (Exp. 7) tests selectivity, and we report specificity/fluency. 6. Behavioral tasks (pronoun/adjective choice) may be prompt-sensitive; we average over multiple paraphrases and report robustness. 7. Findings may be model-specific; three model families address but do not exhaust generality."}

