Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "honorific_register_binding",
  "Title": "Who Am I Talking To? A Causal Study of How Multilingual LLMs Represent Social Register and Bind It to Honorific Grammar",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Multilingual LLMs build a shared, language-agnostic representation of the social relationship between speaker and addressee (relative status/formality), stored separately from surface honorific morphology. When generating in a language that grammatically forces an honorific/register choice (Japanese/Korean honorifics, T-V distinction), the model reads register off this shared 'social-register' variable via a late-layer binding step. We hypothesize (a) an abstract social-register direction that is decodable across languages including register-lax ones like English; (b) that honorific errors in obligatory-honorific languages arise from a binding failure that lazily inherits the register implied by the input/query language rather than re-inferring it from context; and (c) that this inheritance bias scales with how strongly a language grammaticalizes register, not with its resource level, and can be causally repaired by steering the shared register direction.",
  "Related Work": "Prior work studies politeness behaviorally and stylistically (TyDiP 2022; Comparing Styles across Languages 2023; Should We Respect LLMs? 2024) and probes negation/passivization/function words in monolingual BERT-era models (Negation Scope 2020; Controlling the Imprint 2020; Function Words 2022). Interpretability work shows LLMs share cross-lingual concept and grammatical-concept directions (Semantic Hub 2024; Latent Grammatical Concepts 2025) and decode into the target language in late layers. However, all politeness work is behavioral/stylistic and treats register as an output style, while all mechanistic multilingual work targets semantic content or morphological features like gender/number/tense. None isolates the abstract social-relationship variable (relative speaker-addressee status) as a distinct, causally-manipulable representation, none studies the late-layer binding of this variable onto obligatory honorific grammar, and none tests whether register is lazily inherited from the input language. We uniquely treat honorification/T-V typology as a controlled probe of the shared-vs-specific pragmatic representation, give causal (patching + steering) evidence for a register-binding step, and dissociate grammaticalization strength from resource level.",
  "Abstract": "Languages encode the social relationship between speaker and addressee very differently: Japanese and Korean obligatorily mark honorification through verb morphology, Romance/Slavic/German languages force a T-V (informal/formal 'you') choice, and English leaves register largely to lexical and pragmatic cues. Any competent multilingual model must nonetheless recover the same abstract social relationship and, when the target language demands it, commit to a grammatical register. We ask how multilingual LLMs represent this social-register variable and how they bind it onto honorific grammar. We distinguish two representations: an abstract, language-agnostic social-register variable (the relative status/formality of the interlocutors, present regardless of grammatical marking) and the surface honorific/T-V morphology that realizes it. Using parallel dialogues whose social relationship is fixed by context across register-marking and register-lax languages, we train linear probes and localize where register is encoded and where it is bound to grammar. We predict (1) the abstract register direction is decodable across languages, including English; (2) honorific errors in obligatory-honorific languages stem from a late-layer binding failure that lazily inherits the register implied by the query/input language; and (3) this inheritance bias tracks grammaticalization strength, not resource level (e.g., high-resource register-lax English vs. lower-resource register-heavy Korean). We causally test binding via activation patching and repair errors by steering the shared register direction, measuring restored register correctness without harming fluency. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, and Qwen2-7B across 10+ languages. The work provides the first mechanistic, controllable account of pragmatic social register in multilingual LLMs.",
  "Experiments": "1. Stimuli: build parallel dialogue/instruction items whose speaker-addressee social relationship is unambiguously fixed by context (e.g., 'a student writing to a professor' [high formality] vs 'texting a close friend' [low formality]; power/status/intimacy dimensions). Render each item in 10+ languages spanning three register-grammar types: OBLIGATORY-HONORIFIC (Japanese, Korean), T-V DISTINCTION (Spanish, French, German, Russian, Hindi), and REGISTER-LAX (English, Chinese, Indonesian). Include a bare-context variant where relationship must be inferred from lexical cues and an explicit-context variant. Annotate the correct target register per item (native-speaker validation) and a per-language 'register-grammaticalization strength' index (obligatory verb morphology > T-V pronoun > lexical-only). Models: Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, Qwen2-7B. 2. Behavioral register-accuracy baseline: generate replies in each language and measure whether the produced honorific/T-V level matches the context-mandated register; report register-error rate by language type, context strength, and by whether the query/prompt framing language matches or mismatches the generation language. 3. Abstract-register probe: train linear probes at each layer to decode the intended social register (formal/informal, or ordinal register level) from mid-layer hidden states, using held-out items. Show it is decodable in register-lax languages (English/Chinese) too and that a probe trained on one language transfers to others (cross-lingual register-direction sharing). Contrast with a surface-morphology probe (which honorific form appears) to establish the abstract-vs-surface dissociation. 4. Locate the register-binding step (core causal test): run the identical item under context forcing FORMAL vs INFORMAL, producing different honorific outputs in an obligatory-honorific language; activation-patch late-layer components from the formal run into the informal run and identify the minimal set whose patching flips the produced register while preserving message content. Metric: register-flip rate vs number/location of patched components; content-preservation check. 5. Lazy-inheritance test: hold the target generation language fixed (e.g., Japanese) but vary the language/framing in which the context is presented (English formal framing vs Japanese informal-context, etc.); measure how often the output register matches the input-language-implied register rather than the context-mandated register. Prediction: inheritance is strongest when generating obligatory-honorific languages from register-lax input languages. 6. Grammaticalization dose-response: correlate register-error and inheritance rate with the grammaticalization-strength index vs a resource-level proxy (pretraining share / benchmark accuracy). Key dissociation: register-lax high-resource English vs register-heavy lower-resource Korean. Report Spearman with CIs. 7. Causal steering repair: extract a shared social-register direction (mean activation difference formal vs informal, pooled across languages) at the binding layer; on mis-registered items, steer toward the correct register and measure repair rate, and in bare context steer to induce the wrong register (proving functional causality). Metrics: repair rate, induction rate, register correctness, content preservation, output fluency (perplexity), specificity (unrelated attributes unchanged). Compare against an explicit register-instruction prompting baseline and a translate-context-first baseline. 8. Ablations: layer sweep for where register is malleable; probe transfer across models; power vs intimacy dimension separability; whether Japanese/Korean recruit additional late components tied to verb-ending tokens; model scale. Metrics throughout: register-error rate, inheritance rate, probe accuracy, cross-lingual probe transfer, causal flip/repair/induction rates, content preservation, Spearman with grammaticalization vs resource, fluency, specificity.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1. Probe decodability of abstract register may reflect the readout basis rather than a functionally used variable; we require the causal patching flip (Exp. 4) and steering repair/induction (Exp. 7) to establish functional relevance. 2. Ground-truth register in bare-context items can be genuinely ambiguous; we restrict to native-speaker-validated items with high inter-annotator agreement. 3. Separating abstract register from surface morphology is hard in obligatory-honorific languages where they nearly coincide; the register-lax languages and bare-context condition are the crucial dissociating cases. 4. Some models may produce degenerate or under-marked register in low-resource honorific languages (e.g., always default polite), collapsing the effect; quantifying such near-absence of register sensitivity is itself informative, and multiple models/languages give statistical power. 5. Tokenization of honorific verb endings varies across languages; we use verb-final or reply-final token representations and report tokenization statistics; obligatory-honorific vs T-V vs lexical cases are analyzed separately. 6. The grammaticalization-vs-resource dissociation hinges on carefully chosen languages; we report robustness to alternative language choices. 7. Steering may trade register correctness for fluency; we report both. 8. Findings may be model-specific; three model families address but do not exhaust generality."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'honorific_register_binding', 'Title': 'Who Am I Talking To? A Causal Study of How Multilingual LLMs Represent Social Register and Bind It to Honorific Grammar', 'Short Hypothesis': "Multilingual LLMs build a shared, language-agnostic representation of the social relationship between speaker and addressee (relative status/formality), stored separately from surface honorific morphology. When generating in a language that grammatically forces an honorific/register choice (Japanese/Korean honorifics, T-V distinction), the model reads register off this shared 'social-register' variable via a late-layer binding step. We hypothesize (a) an abstract social-register direction that is decodable across languages including register-lax ones like English; (b) that honorific errors in obligatory-honorific languages arise from a binding failure that lazily inherits the register implied by the input/query language rather than re-inferring it from context; and (c) that this inheritance bias scales with how strongly a language grammaticalizes register, not with its resource level, and can be causally repaired by steering the shared register direction.", 'Related Work': 'Prior work studies politeness behaviorally and stylistically (TyDiP 2022; Comparing Styles across Languages 2023; Should We Respect LLMs? 2024) and probes negation/passivization/function words in monolingual BERT-era models (Negation Scope 2020; Controlling the Imprint 2020; Function Words 2022). Interpretability work shows LLMs share cross-lingual concept and grammatical-concept directions (Semantic Hub 2024; Latent Grammatical Concepts 2025) and decode into the target language in late layers. However, all politeness work is behavioral/stylistic and treats register as an output style, while all mechanistic multilingual work targets semantic content or morphological features like gender/number/tense. None isolates the abstract social-relationship variable (relative speaker-addressee status) as a distinct, causally-manipulable representation, none studies the late-layer binding of this variable onto obligatory honorific grammar, and none tests whether register is lazily inherited from the input language. We uniquely treat honorification/T-V typology as a controlled probe of the shared-vs-specific pragmatic representation, give causal (patching + steering) evidence for a register-binding step, and dissociate grammaticalization strength from resource level.', 'Abstract': "Languages encode the social relationship between speaker and addressee very differently: Japanese and Korean obligatorily mark honorification through verb morphology, Romance/Slavic/German languages force a T-V (informal/formal 'you') choice, and English leaves register largely to lexical and pragmatic cues. Any competent multilingual model must nonetheless recover the same abstract social relationship and, when the target language demands it, commit to a grammatical register. We ask how multilingual LLMs represent this social-register variable and how they bind it onto honorific grammar. We distinguish two representations: an abstract, language-agnostic social-register variable (the relative status/formality of the interlocutors, present regardless of grammatical marking) and the surface honorific/T-V morphology that realizes it. Using parallel dialogues whose social relationship is fixed by context across register-marking and register-lax languages, we train linear probes and localize where register is encoded and where it is bound to grammar. We predict (1) the abstract register direction is decodable across languages, including English; (2) honorific errors in obligatory-honorific languages stem from a late-layer binding failure that lazily inherits the register implied by the query/input language; and (3) this inheritance bias tracks grammaticalization strength, not resource level (e.g., high-resource register-lax English vs. lower-resource register-heavy Korean). We causally test binding via activation patching and repair errors by steering the shared register direction, measuring restored register correctness without harming fluency. Experiments span Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, and Qwen2-7B across 10+ languages. The work provides the first mechanistic, controllable account of pragmatic social register in multilingual LLMs.", 'Experiments': "1. Stimuli: build parallel dialogue/instruction items whose speaker-addressee social relationship is unambiguously fixed by context (e.g., 'a student writing to a professor' [high formality] vs 'texting a close friend' [low formality]; power/status/intimacy dimensions). Render each item in 10+ languages spanning three register-grammar types: OBLIGATORY-HONORIFIC (Japanese, Korean), T-V DISTINCTION (Spanish, French, German, Russian, Hindi), and REGISTER-LAX (English, Chinese, Indonesian). Include a bare-context variant where relationship must be inferred from lexical cues and an explicit-context variant. Annotate the correct target register per item (native-speaker validation) and a per-language 'register-grammaticalization strength' index (obligatory verb morphology > T-V pronoun > lexical-only). Models: Llama-3-8B, Aya-23-8B, Qwen2-7B. 2. Behavioral register-accuracy baseline: generate replies in each language and measure whether the produced honorific/T-V level matches the context-mandated register; report register-error rate by language type, context strength, and by whether the query/prompt framing language matches or mismatches the generation language. 3. Abstract-register probe: train linear probes at each layer to decode the intended social register (formal/informal, or ordinal register level) from mid-layer hidden states, using held-out items. Show it is decodable in register-lax languages (English/Chinese) too and that a probe trained on one language transfers to others (cross-lingual register-direction sharing). Contrast with a surface-morphology probe (which honorific form appears) to establish the abstract-vs-surface dissociation. 4. Locate the register-binding step (core causal test): run the identical item under context forcing FORMAL vs INFORMAL, producing different honorific outputs in an obligatory-honorific language; activation-patch late-layer components from the formal run into the informal run and identify the minimal set whose patching flips the produced register while preserving message content. Metric: register-flip rate vs number/location of patched components; content-preservation check. 5. Lazy-inheritance test: hold the target generation language fixed (e.g., Japanese) but vary the language/framing in which the context is presented (English formal framing vs Japanese informal-context, etc.); measure how often the output register matches the input-language-implied register rather than the context-mandated register. Prediction: inheritance is strongest when generating obligatory-honorific languages from register-lax input languages. 6. Grammaticalization dose-response: correlate register-error and inheritance rate with the grammaticalization-strength index vs a resource-level proxy (pretraining share / benchmark accuracy). Key dissociation: register-lax high-resource English vs register-heavy lower-resource Korean. Report Spearman with CIs. 7. Causal steering repair: extract a shared social-register direction (mean activation difference formal vs informal, pooled across languages) at the binding layer; on mis-registered items, steer toward the correct register and measure repair rate, and in bare context steer to induce the wrong register (proving functional causality). Metrics: repair rate, induction rate, register correctness, content preservation, output fluency (perplexity), specificity (unrelated attributes unchanged). Compare against an explicit register-instruction prompting baseline and a translate-context-first baseline. 8. Ablations: layer sweep for where register is malleable; probe transfer across models; power vs intimacy dimension separability; whether Japanese/Korean recruit additional late components tied to verb-ending tokens; model scale. Metrics throughout: register-error rate, inheritance rate, probe accuracy, cross-lingual probe transfer, causal flip/repair/induction rates, content preservation, Spearman with grammaticalization vs resource, fluency, specificity.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1. Probe decodability of abstract register may reflect the readout basis rather than a functionally used variable; we require the causal patching flip (Exp. 4) and steering repair/induction (Exp. 7) to establish functional relevance. 2. Ground-truth register in bare-context items can be genuinely ambiguous; we restrict to native-speaker-validated items with high inter-annotator agreement. 3. Separating abstract register from surface morphology is hard in obligatory-honorific languages where they nearly coincide; the register-lax languages and bare-context condition are the crucial dissociating cases. 4. Some models may produce degenerate or under-marked register in low-resource honorific languages (e.g., always default polite), collapsing the effect; quantifying such near-absence of register sensitivity is itself informative, and multiple models/languages give statistical power. 5. Tokenization of honorific verb endings varies across languages; we use verb-final or reply-final token representations and report tokenization statistics; obligatory-honorific vs T-V vs lexical cases are analyzed separately. 6. The grammaticalization-vs-resource dissociation hinges on carefully chosen languages; we report robustness to alternative language choices. 7. Steering may trade register correctness for fluency; we report both. 8. Findings may be model-specific; three model families address but do not exhaust generality.'}
